    {
      "id": "xs2gx1zVvS-2ls4V5hnni",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -2059.391440218824,
      "y": 1861.9388619477595,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 4250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b8M",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1178410312,
      "version": 6187,
      "versionNonce": 1127013448,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788907312524,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Static post-training quantization\n- wrote a alternative to pytorchin C and C++ with all th ebasic algebra to run depp learning models on\nCPU\n- GGUF is a binary format that stores the quantized weights\n- show the workflow, link to ollama\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDifferent quantization algorithms\n    1. legacy\n    2. K quants\n    3. Q quants\n\nLagacy quants\n- type 0, symmetric -> S\n- type 1, asymmetric -> (S, a)\n- use block quantization, block size is a nob, bigger means less quantization constants (save space) but\nloss granularity. For a block size 32 and a model of 16B need 2B just for the quantizatoin constants\non top of the weights. K solves this.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nK Quants\nthe solution is quantize the quantization constants\nNow the overhead is only 1GB\nThis tw level quantization is also known in the Qlora paper as Double Quantizaton\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nI- Quants\nInterprets goup of weights as vectors in an N dimensinoal space.\n\nIn the image let's see a gorup of three weights.\n\nWe can than create a set of reference vectors (which are the equivlent of the integer bins). Each is\nstored in a codebook which associate it to a a binary code.\nQuantizing the three weights means finding it's nearest reference vector in the codebokk and apply a\nscale to match it's norme. In this way we just have quantized three weights into a binary code.\nCompression!\n\nThis is a lossy opertion. The scale allows us to recover the original magitude of the vector but the\nangular distance between the two vectors is lost. To minimize this loss we need to have more reference\nvectors in the codebook.\n\nGGUF uses 8 dimensional reference vectors.\n\nHow did they choose the vectors and why 8??????\n\nGGUF only have positive refernce vecotrs. So that they can flip all the necgative values in the weight\nand not to loose the sign the have an additional sign vecotr. So a quantizded vector is stored as the\nsum of it's closest reference vecotr plus it's sign vector. In this way the suze of the codebook is\ngrettly reduced.\n\n\nThe compression rate is: (8 weights + 8 sign) / 8 bit = 2bpq\n\nThis does not take into account the scale factor. But becuase I-quants share the same scale for 256\nvectors the overhead is minimal. All of this was for IQ2 quantization. There are many more paradigmns:\n\n\n| Quantization | Nominal bits | Effective bits / weight | Block structure             | Importance matrix |\n| ------------ | -----------: | ----------------------: | --------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| **IQ4_NL**   |        4-bit |  Not stated on the page | Super-blocks of 256 weights | Yes               |\n| **IQ4_XS**   |        4-bit |            **4.25 bpw** | Super-blocks of 256 weights | Yes               |\n| **IQ3_S**    |        3-bit |            **3.44 bpw** | Super-blocks of 256 weights | Yes               |\n| **IQ3_XXS**  |        3-bit |            **3.06 bpw** | Super-blocks of 256 weights | Yes               |\n| **IQ2_S**    |        2-bit |            **2.50 bpw** | Super-blocks of 256 weights | Yes               |\n| **IQ2_XS**   |        2-bit |            **2.31 bpw** | Super-blocks of 256 weights | Yes               |\n| **IQ2_XXS**  |        2-bit |            **2.06 bpw** | Super-blocks of 256 weights | Yes               |\n| **IQ1_M**    |        1-bit |            **1.75 bpw** | Super-blocks of 256 weights | Yes               |\n| **IQ1_S**    |        1-bit |            **1.56 bpw** | Super-blocks of 256 weights | Yes               |\n\n\nThe \"I\" come from Importance matrix. It borrows the idea from --- and AWQ that not all the weights in\nthe model have the same importance. \n\nQuantizatinhappens in two steps:\n\n1. use a calibration dataset to assign an importance score to each row of W. During inference the W is\nmultiplied with X (the calibration datapoint) to produce the activation y. The importance row is given by\nthe squared output activation for that row (AWQ)\n\n\nThe goal is to find the optimal S' and Z' which now are not the same anymore in a way that takes into\naccount the importance matrix. This can be treated as an optmization problem were the loss is given by\nthe squared difference between dequantized and initial weight, weighted by the importance matrix so\nthat more important weight will contribute to the loss more and will have more precise S' and Z'\n\nThe last step is a small grid search around S.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nRecal the improvemebts\n- linear quantization\n- super block quantization\n- vector quant\n\n\n\n\n\nFinally the modifiers. There is amixed precision in the model (show them in an image), the size indicates\nloosly how much precision",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Static post-training quantization\n- wrote a alternative to pytorchin C and C++ with all th ebasic algebra to run depp learning models on CPU\n- GGUF is a binary format that stores the quantized weights\n- show the workflow, link to ollama\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nDifferent quantization algorithms\n    1. legacy\n    2. K quants\n    3. Q quants\n\nLagacy quants\n- type 0, symmetric -> S\n- type 1, asymmetric -> (S, a)\n- use block quantization, block size is a nob, bigger means less quantization constants (save space) but loss granularity. For a block size 32 and a model of 16B need 2B just for the quantizatoin constants on top of the weights. K solves this.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nK Quants\nthe solution is quantize the quantization constants\nNow the overhead is only 1GB\nThis tw level quantization is also known in the Qlora paper as Double Quantizaton\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nI- Quants\nInterprets goup of weights as vectors in an N dimensinoal space.\n\nIn the image let's see a gorup of three weights.\n\nWe can than create a set of reference vectors (which are the equivlent of the integer bins). Each is stored in a codebook which associate it to a a binary code.\nQuantizing the three weights means finding it's nearest reference vector in the codebokk and apply a scale to match it's norme. In this way we just have quantized three weights into a binary code. Compression!\n\nThis is a lossy opertion. The scale allows us to recover the original magitude of the vector but the angular distance between the two vectors is lost. To minimize this loss we need to have more reference vectors in the codebook.\n\nGGUF uses 8 dimensional reference vectors.\n\nHow did they choose the vectors and why 8??????\n\nGGUF only have positive refernce vecotrs. So that they can flip all the necgative values in the weight and not to loose the sign the have an additional sign vecotr. So a quantizded vector is stored as the sum of it's closest reference vecotr plus it's sign vector. In this way the suze of the codebook is grettly reduced.\n\n\nThe compression rate is: (8 weights + 8 sign) / 8 bit = 2bpq\n\nThis does not take into account the scale factor. But becuase I-quants share the same scale for 256 vectors the overhead is minimal. All of this was for IQ2 quantization. There are many more paradigmns:\n\n\n| Quantization | Nominal bits | Effective bits / weight | Block structure             | Importance matrix |\n| ------------ | -----------: | ----------------------: | --------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| **IQ4_NL**   |        4-bit |  Not stated on the page | Super-blocks of 256 weights | Yes               |\n| **IQ4_XS**   |        4-bit |            **4.25 bpw** | Super-blocks of 256 weights | Yes               |\n| **IQ3_S**    |        3-bit |            **3.44 bpw** | Super-blocks of 256 weights | Yes               |\n| **IQ3_XXS**  |        3-bit |            **3.06 bpw** | Super-blocks of 256 weights | Yes               |\n| **IQ2_S**    |        2-bit |            **2.50 bpw** | Super-blocks of 256 weights | Yes               |\n| **IQ2_XS**   |        2-bit |            **2.31 bpw** | Super-blocks of 256 weights | Yes               |\n| **IQ2_XXS**  |        2-bit |            **2.06 bpw** | Super-blocks of 256 weights | Yes               |\n| **IQ1_M**    |        1-bit |            **1.75 bpw** | Super-blocks of 256 weights | Yes               |\n| **IQ1_S**    |        1-bit |            **1.56 bpw** | Super-blocks of 256 weights | Yes               |\n\n\nThe \"I\" come from Importance matrix. It borrows the idea from --- and AWQ that not all the weights in the model have the same importance. \n\nQuantizatinhappens in two steps:\n\n1. use a calibration dataset to assign an importance score to each row of W. During inference the W is multiplied with X (the calibration datapoint) to produce the activation y. The importance row is given by the squared output activation for that row (AWQ)\n\n\nThe goal is to find the optimal S' and Z' which now are not the same anymore in a way that takes into account the importance matrix. This can be treated as an optmization problem were the loss is given by the squared difference between dequantized and initial weight, weighted by the importance matrix so that more important weight will contribute to the loss more and will have more precise S' and Z'\n\nThe last step is a small grid search around S.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nRecal the improvemebts\n- linear quantization\n- super block quantization\n- vector quant\n\n\n\n\n\nFinally the modifiers. There is amixed precision in the model (show them in an image), the size indicates loosly how much precision",
