    {
      "id": "kssvcpISROhvoeZqegUAl",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -12924.431510775858,
      "y": 3028.93386774146,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "c582vxAEYuS_QhCQPeYjB"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bEN",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 459709256,
      "version": 800,
      "versionNonce": 2135075656,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788957880468,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "A common way to  representing real numbers is through floating-point: the number of digits for\nrepresenting the integer and the fractional parts can vary. \n    - FP16 (half-precision)\n    - FP32 (full-precision)\n    - bFP16\n\nThere are three components in the floating-point representation:\n    1. The sign bit: this is the bit specifying the sign of the number.\n    2. The exponent part\n    3. The mantissa/fraction/significant",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "A common way to  representing real numbers is through floating-point: the number of digits for representing the integer and the fractional parts can vary. \n    - FP16 (half-precision)\n    - FP32 (full-precision)\n    - bFP16\n\nThere are three components in the floating-point representation:\n    1. The sign bit: this is the bit specifying the sign of the number.\n    2. The exponent part\n    3. The mantissa/fraction/significant",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "3eS0HzDjwj5ECJ4DshO8Z",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -12372.187969243749,
      "y": 7306.1637341288715,
      "width": 206.4728546142578,
      "height": 35,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "MWUkp6JscsuMnuBMw3_4I"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bEO",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1501819464,
      "version": 1405,
      "versionNonce": 123601480,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788957880468,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Dequantization",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Dequantization",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "nNDjVJk5vhks_7Hf78-gI",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -12788.566223413238,
      "y": 7365.355734620132,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 2700,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "MWUkp6JscsuMnuBMw3_4I"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bEP",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 937322824,
      "version": 5501,
      "versionNonce": 1073117512,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "CqvR2d77_j7dk7D6gk2OO",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1788957880468,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Quantizing is only part of the story. -> running inference menas executing menas operation that also\nneed to convert integers into floats\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nHowever if we see in the formula there is still a moltiplication with a real number S which are the kind of\noperations we want to avoid. \n\nThe trick is in Fixed Point Arithmetic: a way to represent fractions only through integers.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWe store the full value without dot, and keep track of the scale ->  to recover the original values we\nwould have to divide by 100. This is also why it is called \"Fixed\" Point Arithmetic: in contrast to\nfloating-points, where the point can effectively move because the number stores an exponent, here the\npoint is always in the same place given the constant scale.\n\nSo now we could store:\n                                                    1234  → 12.34\n                                                    5678  → 56.78\n                                                       5  → 0.05\n                                                    \nAll numbers use the same fixed scale 0.01\n\nIn practice, because computers are binary machines, we do not use the sclae in base 10 but in base 2,\nand if we have an unsigned int to represent the scale (to optimize for memory usage) the smallest step\nwe can apply at at INT4 is 2^(-4)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nNow, how we use FPA to multiply an integer with a real value and get an integer?\n\n\n\nA naive solution could be store S as fixed point number and perfom the multiplication with fixed point\narithmic...\n\nSuppose you have X = 1000 from an INT8 matrix multiplication and you need to multiply it by a real\nnumber S = 0.3\n\nMathematically: 1000 x 0.3 = 300\n\nIf we want integer-only arithmetic, we do not want the processor to literally multiply an integer by the\nfloating-point number. Instead, we approximate 0.3 using a fixed-point integer plus a power of two.\n\n                0.3 × 24 = 4.8 -> 5\n\nNow our original operation becomes approximately\n\n1000×5×2^(−4) = 312.5\n\nTwo problems with this:\n- there is a loss in precision (which can be mitigated using langer bits for the scale, with 8 fractional\nbits we would have obtained 300.78, but at the double of memory usage)\n- if the scale is very small it aproximate to zero\n\nIf it was this easy we wouldn't bother with quantization!\n\nLet's say we have S = 0.003 with the same scale 2^(-4)\n\n0.003 × 24 = 0.048 -> 0\n\nwe can't come back from this and any integer multiplied by this will be zero.\n\nThis is the sulution:\n\n\n\nThis is done before deployment. The purpose is to move the very small magnitude of \\(S\\) into the\npower-of-two term, while keeping \\(S_0\\) reasonably large.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAs an optimizatin we can precompute the decomposition of S once before deploying the model.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Quantizing is only part of the story. -> running inference menas executing menas operation that also need to convert integers into floats\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nHowever if we see in the formula there is still a moltiplication with a real number S which are the kind of operations we want to avoid. \n\nThe trick is in Fixed Point Arithmetic: a way to represent fractions only through integers.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWe store the full value without dot, and keep track of the scale ->  to recover the original values we would have to divide by 100. This is also why it is called \"Fixed\" Point Arithmetic: in contrast to floating-points, where the point can effectively move because the number stores an exponent, here the point is always in the same place given the constant scale.\n\nSo now we could store:\n                                                    1234  → 12.34\n                                                    5678  → 56.78\n                                                       5  → 0.05\n                                                    \nAll numbers use the same fixed scale 0.01\n\nIn practice, because computers are binary machines, we do not use the sclae in base 10 but in base 2, and if we have an unsigned int to represent the scale (to optimize for memory usage) the smallest step we can apply at at INT4 is 2^(-4)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nNow, how we use FPA to multiply an integer with a real value and get an integer?\n\n\n\nA naive solution could be store S as fixed point number and perfom the multiplication with fixed point arithmic...\n\nSuppose you have X = 1000 from an INT8 matrix multiplication and you need to multiply it by a real number S = 0.3\n\nMathematically: 1000 x 0.3 = 300\n\nIf we want integer-only arithmetic, we do not want the processor to literally multiply an integer by the floating-point number. Instead, we approximate 0.3 using a fixed-point integer plus a power of two.\n\n                0.3 × 24 = 4.8 -> 5\n\nNow our original operation becomes approximately\n\n1000×5×2^(−4) = 312.5\n\nTwo problems with this:\n- there is a loss in precision (which can be mitigated using langer bits for the scale, with 8 fractional bits we would have obtained 300.78, but at the double of memory usage)\n- if the scale is very small it aproximate to zero\n\nIf it was this easy we wouldn't bother with quantization!\n\nLet's say we have S = 0.003 with the same scale 2^(-4)\n\n0.003 × 24 = 0.048 -> 0\n\nwe can't come back from this and any integer multiplied by this will be zero.\n\nThis is the sulution:\n\n\n\nThis is done before deployment. The purpose is to move the very small magnitude of \\(S\\) into the power-of-two term, while keeping \\(S_0\\) reasonably large.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAs an optimizatin we can precompute the decomposition of S once before deploying the model.\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
