    {
      "id": "SxCXJBi-rEJ_1s_KcffdR",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -10739.921558030104,
      "y": 3553.488007930857,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 6650,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "hlr7y_9P90BALovRla4YJ"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bFA",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2129609288,
      "version": 2842,
      "versionNonce": 1206183240,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788957880468,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- dynamic post-training quantization\n- quantization primitives for INT8 (weights and activations)\n\n\nThe Importance of outlier features\nThe authors discovered that some activation dimensions contain extremely large values, which they call\noutlier features.\n\nAbove 6B outliers are:\n1. extremely sparse. there can be a huge number of individual outlier values across a sequence, but they\nare concentrated in only a handful of hidden dimensions. The paper reports about 150,000 outlier\noccurrences in a 2048-token sequence for a 6.7B model, concentrated in only 6 hidden dimensions.\n\n2. much larger than ordinary activations. The paper observes feature values that can be roughly 20×\nlarger than values in normal dimensions. As models scale, these extreme magnitudes grow substantially.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n3. ubiquitus. Around that point, outliers become present in essentially all Transformer layers and affect\na large fraction of token positions. This is also where conventional INT8 quantization begins to\ndegrade sharply. They are therefore a structural property of the representation, not isolated numerical\naccidents. the percentage of layers affected by outlier features rises to essentially 100%\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n4. associated strongly with attention-related representations. the authors find these outlier features\nin attention projections—key, query, value, and output—and in the first/expansion linear layer of the\nfeed-forward network. So they are not uniformly distributed over every operation in the Transformer.\n\n5. systematic. The outliers repeatedly occur in the same hidden dimensions across tokens and layers.\nThat is why the authors can identify a small set:\n\n\n                                Activation tensor across Transformer layers\n                                Rows = token positions\n                                Columns = hidden dimensions\n                                \n                                Layer 1\n                                token 1       .  .  .  .  X  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                token 2       .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                token 3       .  .  .  .  X  .  .  .  .  .   .   .   .   .\n                                token 4       .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                \n                                Layer 2\n                                token 1       .  .  .  .  X  .  .  .  .  .   .   .   .   .\n                                token 2       .  .  .  .  X  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                token 3       .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .   .   .   .   .\n                                token 4       .  .  .  .  X  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                \n                                Layer 3\n                                token 1       .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                token 2       .  .  .  .  X  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                token 3       .  .  .  .  X  .  .  .  .  .   .   .   .   .\n                                token 4       .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                \n                                Layer 4\n                                token 1       .  .  .  .  X  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                token 2       .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                token 3       .  .  .  .  X  .  .  .  .  .   .   .   .   .\n                                token 4       .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n\n\n6. not noise but crucial. These outliers are critical for transformer performance. If the outliers are\nremoved, the mean top-1 softmax probability is reduced from about 40% to about 20%, and validation\nperplexity increases by 600-1000% even though there are at most 7 outlier feature dimensions. When we\nremove 7 random feature dimensions instead, the top-1 probability decreases only between 0.02-0.3%,\nand perplexity increases by 0.1%. This highlights the critical nature of these feature dimensions.\nQuantization precision for these outlier features is paramount as even tiny errors greatly impact model\nperformance.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis is the core reason why quantization methods fail starting at the 6B scale – the range of the\nquantization distribution is too large so that most quantization bins are empty and small quantization\nvalues are quantized to zero, essentially extinguishing information.\n\n\nThe Ideas: \n1. Mixed-precision decomposition. We said that that some activation dimensions contain very large\noutliers. If we quantize those weight dimensions, they'll distort the scale. So the authors split the\nmatrix multiplication between activations (or input in case of the first layer) and the model weights into\ntwo parts. Given 16-bit floating-point inputs Xf 16 and weights Wf 16 , the features and weights are\ndecomposed into sub-matrices of large magnitude features and other values. The outlier feature\nmatrices are multiplied in 16-bit. All other values are multiplied in 8-bit. In inference, the last values are\ndequantized.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n2. Vector-wise quantization: use diffreent scaling factors when multiplying input and weight tensors.  We\nperform 8-bit vector-wise multiplication by scaling by row and column-wise absolute maximum of\nCx and Cw and then quantizing the outputs to Int8. The Int32 matrix multiplication outputs Outi32\nare dequantization by the outer product of the normalization constants Cx ⊗ Cw . Finally, both outlier\nand regular outputs are accumulated in 16-bit floating point outputs. Instead of using one scale for the\nentire activation matrix and one for the entire weight matrix, the paper uses different scaling constants\nfor different vectors.\n\n                                                        X activations\n                                            \n                                            col 1: [45    12   37]   → scale sx1\n                                            col 2: [-17  -63  -83]   → scale sx2\n\n\n\n\nHunt for Outlier Features\nThey used an empirical approach to find outliers ans define them according to the following criteria: \n\n1. the magnitude of the featur is at least 6.0, \n2. affects at least 25% of layers, \n3. and affects at least 6% of the sequence dimensions.\n\n\nThe magnitude\n                                                       hidden dimensions\n                                                     h0   h1   h2   h3   h4\n                                        \n                                        token 1      .4   .2   .7   .3   8.1  ← outlier\n                                        token 2      .1   .5   .4   .2   7.3\n                                        token 3      .3   .1   .8   .4   0.9\n\n\nThey experimented with the mixed-precision decomposition and observed how model perplexity changed\nwhen increasingly large activations were treated as outliers. They found that perplexity degradation\nessentially stopped when every feature with magnitude 6 or greater was handled as an outlier. That\nempirical observation became the cutoff.\n\nThe same feature dimension must be affected in ≥25% of layers\n\n                                                                  h4\n                                                \n                                                Layer 1           X\n                                                Layer 2           .\n                                                Layer 3           X\n                                                Layer 4           X\n                                                Layer 5           .\n                                                Layer 6           X\n                                                ...\n                                                Layer 40          X\n\nFor this criterion, they studied how often the same hidden feature dimension contained an outlier\nacross transformer layers.\n\nThey noticed an important difference:\n\n- in large models, outlier dimensions are highly systematic and they tend to appear in most layers or not\nat all;\n- in small models, they are more probabilistic and can occur sporadically in a few layers.\n\nSo they chose the 125M model because it was the smallest model in their experimental set, and\ntherefore the most conservative place to calibrate the thresholds.\n\nThe logic is this: in small models, outlier behavior is much more sporadic and probabilistic. The same\nhidden dimension may show a large activation in a few layers just by chance. In large models, by\ncontrast, the outlier dimensions become much more systematic and tend to appear across many layers.\nThe authors explicitly make this distinction.\n\nSo they used the 125M model as a kind of stress test for false positives. They wanted a threshold\nthat was strict enough that random, occasional large activations in the smallest model would not all be\nclassified as meaningful outlier features.\n\n\n                                frequency across layers\n                                \n                                25%   ████████████████████████   candidate #1\n                                  2%   ██                                          candidate #2\n                                 ~0%  ▏                                            others\n\n\nThe same feature dimension must be affected in ≥6% of the sequence dimensions (tokens)\n\n                                                                        h4\n                                                    \n                                                    Token 1             X\n                                                    Token 2             X\n                                                    Token 3             .\n                                                    Token 4             .\n                                                    Token 5             X\n                                                    Token 6             .\n                                                    Token 7             .\n                                                    Token 8             X\n                                                    Token 9             .\n                                                    Token 10            .\n                                                    ...\n                                                    Token 100           X\n                                                    \n                                                                        ↑\n                                                               ≥ 6% of all tokens\n                                                    \nThey applied the same calibration procedure to the sequence dimension. with the 125M model.\n\n\nResults\n- 8bit quantization vs FP16: 2x memory saving, accuracy on par\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- dynamic post-training quantization\n- quantization primitives for INT8 (weights and activations)\n\n\nThe Importance of outlier features\nThe authors discovered that some activation dimensions contain extremely large values, which they call outlier features.\n\nAbove 6B outliers are:\n1. extremely sparse. there can be a huge number of individual outlier values across a sequence, but they are concentrated in only a handful of hidden dimensions. The paper reports about 150,000 outlier occurrences in a 2048-token sequence for a 6.7B model, concentrated in only 6 hidden dimensions.\n\n2. much larger than ordinary activations. The paper observes feature values that can be roughly 20× larger than values in normal dimensions. As models scale, these extreme magnitudes grow substantially.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n3. ubiquitus. Around that point, outliers become present in essentially all Transformer layers and affect a large fraction of token positions. This is also where conventional INT8 quantization begins to degrade sharply. They are therefore a structural property of the representation, not isolated numerical accidents. the percentage of layers affected by outlier features rises to essentially 100%\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n4. associated strongly with attention-related representations. the authors find these outlier features in attention projections—key, query, value, and output—and in the first/expansion linear layer of the feed-forward network. So they are not uniformly distributed over every operation in the Transformer.\n\n5. systematic. The outliers repeatedly occur in the same hidden dimensions across tokens and layers. That is why the authors can identify a small set:\n\n\n                                Activation tensor across Transformer layers\n                                Rows = token positions\n                                Columns = hidden dimensions\n                                \n                                Layer 1\n                                token 1       .  .  .  .  X  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                token 2       .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                token 3       .  .  .  .  X  .  .  .  .  .   .   .   .   .\n                                token 4       .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                \n                                Layer 2\n                                token 1       .  .  .  .  X  .  .  .  .  .   .   .   .   .\n                                token 2       .  .  .  .  X  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                token 3       .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .   .   .   .   .\n                                token 4       .  .  .  .  X  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                \n                                Layer 3\n                                token 1       .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                token 2       .  .  .  .  X  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                token 3       .  .  .  .  X  .  .  .  .  .   .   .   .   .\n                                token 4       .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                \n                                Layer 4\n                                token 1       .  .  .  .  X  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                token 2       .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n                                token 3       .  .  .  .  X  .  .  .  .  .   .   .   .   .\n                                token 4       .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .  .   .   X   .   .\n\n\n6. not noise but crucial. These outliers are critical for transformer performance. If the outliers are removed, the mean top-1 softmax probability is reduced from about 40% to about 20%, and validation perplexity increases by 600-1000% even though there are at most 7 outlier feature dimensions. When we remove 7 random feature dimensions instead, the top-1 probability decreases only between 0.02-0.3%, and perplexity increases by 0.1%. This highlights the critical nature of these feature dimensions. Quantization precision for these outlier features is paramount as even tiny errors greatly impact model performance.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThis is the core reason why quantization methods fail starting at the 6B scale – the range of the quantization distribution is too large so that most quantization bins are empty and small quantization values are quantized to zero, essentially extinguishing information.\n\n\nThe Ideas: \n1. Mixed-precision decomposition. We said that that some activation dimensions contain very large outliers. If we quantize those weight dimensions, they'll distort the scale. So the authors split the matrix multiplication between activations (or input in case of the first layer) and the model weights into two parts. Given 16-bit floating-point inputs Xf 16 and weights Wf 16 , the features and weights are decomposed into sub-matrices of large magnitude features and other values. The outlier feature matrices are multiplied in 16-bit. All other values are multiplied in 8-bit. In inference, the last values are dequantized.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n2. Vector-wise quantization: use diffreent scaling factors when multiplying input and weight tensors.  We\nperform 8-bit vector-wise multiplication by scaling by row and column-wise absolute maximum of\nCx and Cw and then quantizing the outputs to Int8. The Int32 matrix multiplication outputs Outi32\nare dequantization by the outer product of the normalization constants Cx ⊗ Cw . Finally, both outlier\nand regular outputs are accumulated in 16-bit floating point outputs. Instead of using one scale for the entire activation matrix and one for the entire weight matrix, the paper uses different scaling constants for different vectors.\n\n                                                        X activations\n                                            \n                                            col 1: [45    12   37]   → scale sx1\n                                            col 2: [-17  -63  -83]   → scale sx2\n\n\n\n\nHunt for Outlier Features\nThey used an empirical approach to find outliers ans define them according to the following criteria: \n\n1. the magnitude of the featur is at least 6.0, \n2. affects at least 25% of layers, \n3. and affects at least 6% of the sequence dimensions.\n\n\nThe magnitude\n                                                       hidden dimensions\n                                                     h0   h1   h2   h3   h4\n                                        \n                                        token 1      .4   .2   .7   .3   8.1  ← outlier\n                                        token 2      .1   .5   .4   .2   7.3\n                                        token 3      .3   .1   .8   .4   0.9\n\n\nThey experimented with the mixed-precision decomposition and observed how model perplexity changed when increasingly large activations were treated as outliers. They found that perplexity degradation essentially stopped when every feature with magnitude 6 or greater was handled as an outlier. That empirical observation became the cutoff.\n\nThe same feature dimension must be affected in ≥25% of layers\n\n                                                                  h4\n                                                \n                                                Layer 1           X\n                                                Layer 2           .\n                                                Layer 3           X\n                                                Layer 4           X\n                                                Layer 5           .\n                                                Layer 6           X\n                                                ...\n                                                Layer 40          X\n\nFor this criterion, they studied how often the same hidden feature dimension contained an outlier across transformer layers.\n\nThey noticed an important difference:\n\n- in large models, outlier dimensions are highly systematic and they tend to appear in most layers or not at all;\n- in small models, they are more probabilistic and can occur sporadically in a few layers.\n\nSo they chose the 125M model because it was the smallest model in their experimental set, and therefore the most conservative place to calibrate the thresholds.\n\nThe logic is this: in small models, outlier behavior is much more sporadic and probabilistic. The same hidden dimension may show a large activation in a few layers just by chance. In large models, by contrast, the outlier dimensions become much more systematic and tend to appear across many layers. The authors explicitly make this distinction.\n\nSo they used the 125M model as a kind of stress test for false positives. They wanted a threshold that was strict enough that random, occasional large activations in the smallest model would not all be classified as meaningful outlier features.\n\n\n                                frequency across layers\n                                \n                                25%   ████████████████████████   candidate #1\n                                  2%   ██                                          candidate #2\n                                 ~0%  ▏                                            others\n\n\nThe same feature dimension must be affected in ≥6% of the sequence dimensions (tokens)\n\n                                                                        h4\n                                                    \n                                                    Token 1             X\n                                                    Token 2             X\n                                                    Token 3             .\n                                                    Token 4             .\n                                                    Token 5             X\n                                                    Token 6             .\n                                                    Token 7             .\n                                                    Token 8             X\n                                                    Token 9             .\n                                                    Token 10            .\n                                                    ...\n                                                    Token 100           X\n                                                    \n                                                                        ↑\n                                                               ≥ 6% of all tokens\n                                                    \nThey applied the same calibration procedure to the sequence dimension. with the 125M model.\n\n\nResults\n- 8bit quantization vs FP16: 2x memory saving, accuracy on par\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
