    },
    {
      "id": "wAq_CTPRg58ayHgN25SEB",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -7925.047658820386,
      "y": 2042.690657003921,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 1875,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "4xPlj53adrwpZwUt4JR5i",
        "IpISbTxy6zVwiZHtwkv_C"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bHH",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 995479864,
      "version": 3313,
      "versionNonce": 1669443896,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1788970621257,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Static post-training quantization\n- achieves new levels of qualitaziones: 8, 4, but also 3 and 2 (weight-only)\n\n\nSo LLM.int8() asks: Which feature dimensions are dangerous for quantization, and how can we isolate\nthem?\n\n                                            Hidden activations X\n                                                       │\n                                                       ▼\n                                                Detect outlier dimensions hi\n                                                       │\n                                                       ├───────────────┐\n                                                       │                           │\n                                                regular dimensions   outlier dimensions\n                                                       │                           │\n                                                       ▼                         ▼\n                                                     INT8                    FP16\n                                                       │                           │\n                                                      ▼                          ▼\n                                                corresponding            corresponding\n                                                weights → INT8      weights → FP16\n                                                       │                           │\n                                                       └───────┬───────┘\n                                                                     ▼\n                                                                add outputs\n\nInt8 can work well with techniques such as LLM.int8(). But what if we want to compress the weights\nmuch more aggressively—to 4 bits or 3 bits? \n\nThe Problem\nAt lower quantization the error due to the quant/dequant operation completely destroying the layer\noutput! First of all let's see with an example what is this error.\nWhen you quantize a float weight to an integer, you do not compare the float directly to the raw\ninteger code. You compare it to the dequantized value represented by that integer.\nSuppose\n\n\n\nand the quantization scale is\n\n\n\nYou first compute the integer code:\n\n\n\n\nSo the stored value is the integer\n\n\n\nBut that integer represents the floating-point value\n\n\n\n\nTherefore the quantization error is\n\n\n\n\n\nNow this error summed for all the weghts is too much a lower quantizations...\n\n\n\nThe Optimization Problem\nThe authors of AWQ frame qantization as an optimization problem where the objective is:\n\n\n\n\nor rather\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Static post-training quantization\n- achieves new levels of qualitaziones: 8, 4, but also 3 and 2 (weight-only)\n\n\nSo LLM.int8() asks: Which feature dimensions are dangerous for quantization, and how can we isolate them?\n\n                                            Hidden activations X\n                                                       │\n                                                       ▼\n                                                Detect outlier dimensions hi\n                                                       │\n                                                       ├───────────────┐\n                                                       │                           │\n                                                regular dimensions   outlier dimensions\n                                                       │                           │\n                                                       ▼                         ▼\n                                                     INT8                    FP16\n                                                       │                           │\n                                                      ▼                          ▼\n                                                corresponding            corresponding\n                                                weights → INT8      weights → FP16\n                                                       │                           │\n                                                       └───────┬───────┘\n                                                                     ▼\n                                                                add outputs\n\nInt8 can work well with techniques such as LLM.int8(). But what if we want to compress the weights much more aggressively—to 4 bits or 3 bits? \n\nThe Problem\nAt lower quantization the error due to the quant/dequant operation completely destroying the layer output! First of all let's see with an example what is this error.\nWhen you quantize a float weight to an integer, you do not compare the float directly to the raw integer code. You compare it to the dequantized value represented by that integer.\nSuppose\n\n\n\nand the quantization scale is\n\n\n\nYou first compute the integer code:\n\n\n\n\nSo the stored value is the integer\n\n\n\nBut that integer represents the floating-point value\n\n\n\n\nTherefore the quantization error is\n\n\n\n\n\nNow this error summed for all the weghts is too much a lower quantizations...\n\n\n\nThe Optimization Problem\nThe authors of AWQ frame qantization as an optimization problem where the objective is:\n\n\n\n\nor rather\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
