    {
      "id": "AmZoVg_pI37YeB4MsOnyn",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -7921.507869637571,
      "y": 4130.004596980198,
      "width": 1046.5712193080344,
      "height": 6450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "9f2qFwu1Ba9ziJBRX91i4",
        "ws5GxG--fNsmr1KTKY-ae"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bHV",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2103496504,
      "version": 1154,
      "versionNonce": 1631891256,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "YFl-2JMPJ7D-akJDX9mUZ",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1788970621257,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Update column by column\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nEach row produces one output:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nevery weight in column 1 multiplies the same input feature x\n\n                                               x1\n                                                │\n                                                ▼\n                            \n                                               col 1\n                            \n                                                w11  ──>  x1\n                                                w21  ──>  x1\n                                                w31  ──>  x1\n\nThat is why columns matter to GPTQ.\n\nAt each step:\n1. one column is quantized\n2. Hessian is used to adjust the other weights\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nInverse Hessian\nA Hessian is a matrix of second derivatives. It tells you how the slope of a function is changing in\ndifferent directions. For a scalar function:\n\n\n\n\nthe Hessian is\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn GPTQ, the Hessian tells you how sensitive the layer output error is to perturbing the weights. The\ndiagonal tells you how much each input feature varies or contributes on calibration data. The off-diagonal\ntells you how much two input features move together.\n\nIntuitively:\n\n    - a large Hessian value/direction means the loss rises quickly if you move the weights there\n    - a small Hessian value/direction means the loss is relatively flat, so quantization is less harmful\n\nGPTQ uses this curvature information not just to estimate the cost of rounding a weight, but also to\ndecide how the remaining weights should be adjusted to compensate for that quantization error. In the\npaper, this curvature comes from the layer inputs through\n\n\n\n\nLet's make an example. Suppose we quantize a weight in column 1 and create an error\n\n\n\n\nGPTQ then asks: Can changes in the remaining weights w2, w3, etc compensate for the lost contribution\ne1\n\nIf calibration shows that x2 behaves very similarly to x1, then changing w2 can compensate well. If x3 is\nunrelated to x1, changing w3 is much less useful.\n\n\n                                                w1 quantized\n                                                     ↓\n                                                lost contribution involving x1\n                                                     ↓\n                                                \n                                                Who can compensate?\n                                                \n                                                w2 × x2   ✓ useful, because x2 ≈ x1\n                                                w3 × x3   ✗ less useful\n\n\nThe inverse Hessian \\(H^{-1}\\) is what GPTQ uses to compute the actual adjustment. In the OBQ/GPTQ\nderivation, after quantizing weight \\(w_q\\), the remaining weights are updated approximately as\n\n\n\n\nwhere:\n    - F: the set of weights that are still full precision / not yet quantized\n    - Q: the set of weights before being quantized in the current block\n    - wQ: the vector of weights whose indices are in Q\n    - quant(wQ): those same weights after quantization\n    - wQ - quant(wQ): the quantization error\n    - H_F: inverse Hessian restricted to the still-unquantized weights F\n\n\nNow the full algorithm.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nHere B is the block size, meaning the number of consecutive weight columns GPTQ processes together\nbefore applying a larger update to the rest of the matrix. This because a direct implementation of the\nscheme described previously will not be fast in practice, because the algorithm has a relatively low\ncompute-to-memory-access ratio. Theupdate equation needs to update all elements of a potentially huge\nmatrix using just a few FLOPs for each entry. Such operations cannot properly utilize the massive\ncompute capabilities of modern GPUs, and will be bottlenecked by the significantly lower memory\nbandwidth.\n\nConcretely, we apply the algorithm to B = 128 columns at a time, keeping updates contained to those\ncolumns and the corresponding B × B block of H−1. Only once a block has been fully processed, we perform\nglobal updates of the entire H−1 and W matrices.\n\n                                            block 1: columns   0 ... 127\n                                            block 2: columns 128 ... 255\n                                            block 3: columns 256 ... 383\n                                            ...\n\nInside each block, the inner loop quantizes one column at a time and updates the still-unquantized weights\ninside that block. Then, once the entire block is finished, GPTQ applies the accumulated block error E to\nthe columns after the block:\n\n\n                                        current block\n                                        ┌─────────┐\n                                        │ c1 c2 c3 c4  │ | c5 c6 c7 c8 ...\n                                        └─────────┘\n                                              ↑\n                                           quantize here\n\nworking inside the block. But the errors from c1,c2,c3,c4 also matter for the later columns. So after the\nblock is complete, GPTQ performs one larger update:\n\n                           \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nGPTQ is doing two levels of compensation:\n\n    - compensation inside the current block\n    - compensation from the finished block to all later columns\n\nThey call this optimization Lazy Batch-Updates.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Update column by column\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nEach row produces one output:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nevery weight in column 1 multiplies the same input feature x\n\n                                               x1\n                                                │\n                                                ▼\n                            \n                                               col 1\n                            \n                                                w11  ──>  x1\n                                                w21  ──>  x1\n                                                w31  ──>  x1\n\nThat is why columns matter to GPTQ.\n\nAt each step:\n1. one column is quantized\n2. Hessian is used to adjust the other weights\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nInverse Hessian\nA Hessian is a matrix of second derivatives. It tells you how the slope of a function is changing in different directions. For a scalar function:\n\n\n\n\nthe Hessian is\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIn GPTQ, the Hessian tells you how sensitive the layer output error is to perturbing the weights. The diagonal tells you how much each input feature varies or contributes on calibration data. The off-diagonal tells you how much two input features move together.\n\nIntuitively:\n\n    - a large Hessian value/direction means the loss rises quickly if you move the weights there\n    - a small Hessian value/direction means the loss is relatively flat, so quantization is less harmful\n\nGPTQ uses this curvature information not just to estimate the cost of rounding a weight, but also to decide how the remaining weights should be adjusted to compensate for that quantization error. In the paper, this curvature comes from the layer inputs through\n\n\n\n\nLet's make an example. Suppose we quantize a weight in column 1 and create an error\n\n\n\n\nGPTQ then asks: Can changes in the remaining weights w2, w3, etc compensate for the lost contribution e1\n\nIf calibration shows that x2 behaves very similarly to x1, then changing w2 can compensate well. If x3 is unrelated to x1, changing w3 is much less useful.\n\n\n                                                w1 quantized\n                                                     ↓\n                                                lost contribution involving x1\n                                                     ↓\n                                                \n                                                Who can compensate?\n                                                \n                                                w2 × x2   ✓ useful, because x2 ≈ x1\n                                                w3 × x3   ✗ less useful\n\n\nThe inverse Hessian \\(H^{-1}\\) is what GPTQ uses to compute the actual adjustment. In the OBQ/GPTQ derivation, after quantizing weight \\(w_q\\), the remaining weights are updated approximately as\n\n\n\n\nwhere:\n    - F: the set of weights that are still full precision / not yet quantized\n    - Q: the set of weights before being quantized in the current block\n    - wQ: the vector of weights whose indices are in Q\n    - quant(wQ): those same weights after quantization\n    - wQ - quant(wQ): the quantization error\n    - H_F: inverse Hessian restricted to the still-unquantized weights F\n\n\nNow the full algorithm.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nHere B is the block size, meaning the number of consecutive weight columns GPTQ processes together before applying a larger update to the rest of the matrix. This because a direct implementation of the scheme described previously will not be fast in practice, because the algorithm has a relatively low compute-to-memory-access ratio. Theupdate equation needs to update all elements of a potentially huge matrix using just a few FLOPs for each entry. Such operations cannot properly utilize the massive compute capabilities of modern GPUs, and will be bottlenecked by the significantly lower memory bandwidth.\n\nConcretely, we apply the algorithm to B = 128 columns at a time, keeping updates contained to those columns and the corresponding B × B block of H−1. Only once a block has been fully processed, we perform global updates of the entire H−1 and W matrices.\n\n                                            block 1: columns   0 ... 127\n                                            block 2: columns 128 ... 255\n                                            block 3: columns 256 ... 383\n                                            ...\n\nInside each block, the inner loop quantizes one column at a time and updates the still-unquantized weights inside that block. Then, once the entire block is finished, GPTQ applies the accumulated block error E to the columns after the block:\n\n\n                                        current block\n                                        ┌─────────┐\n                                        │ c1 c2 c3 c4  │ | c5 c6 c7 c8 ...\n                                        └─────────┘\n                                              ↑\n                                           quantize here\n\nworking inside the block. But the errors from c1,c2,c3,c4 also matter for the later columns. So after the block is complete, GPTQ performs one larger update:\n\n                           \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nGPTQ is doing two levels of compensation:\n\n    - compensation inside the current block\n    - compensation from the finished block to all later columns\n\nThey call this optimization Lazy Batch-Updates.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
