      "text": "Searching to scale",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Searching to scale",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "PRH9FhIR3xXB5_tkGwrvj",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -4973.048108978412,
      "y": 5724.774780034124,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 1725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "07ABpJuq92tsq1td0e-uw",
        "uXSKSHyQ8UaE2a01v9yjm"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bPA",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1863997754,
      "version": 3443,
      "versionNonce": 2001112806,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789027095060,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Now they asked temselves: how much should we multiply salient weights?\nThey choose to automatically search for an optimal (per input channel) scaling factor that minimizes the\noutput difference after quantization for a certain layer. Formally, we want to optimize the following\nobjective:\n\n\n\n\n\n\nwhich in other words means:\n\n\n\n\nSimilarly to what we said in GPTQ, also here the author care for finding a scale for the entier channel,\nthat is why the diag(s)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe activations are insteda scaled down but the same reasoning applies.\n\nSince the quantization function is not differentiable, they were not able to directly optimize the problem\nwith vanilla backpropagation. So ther define a search space for the optimal scale by analyzing the\nfactors that will affect the choice of scaling factor. \nBut a layer with 4096 input channels would have:\n\n\n\nmeaning 4096 independent optimization variables. That is expensive!\n\nAWQ's trick is: given that the saliency of weight channels is actually determined by the activation scale\n(thus “activation-awareness”) do not search over arbitrary scales but build the scales directly from\nactivation statistics.\n\nThey compute:\n\n\n\nwhich is the average magnitude of activation (per-channel), and they use a single hyper-parameter α to\nbalance between the protection of salient and non-salient channels. \n\n\n\n\nThey can find the best α by a fast grid search over the interval of [0, 1] (0 means we do not scale; 1\ncorresponds to the most aggressive scaling in our search space).\n\nThe whole algorithm looks like this:\n\n    1. Calibration data\n    2. measure activation magnitude\n    3. sX\n    4. try different α values\n    5. s = sX^α\n    6. quantize weights\n    7. measure output error\n    8. choose best α\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Now they asked temselves: how much should we multiply salient weights?\nThey choose to automatically search for an optimal (per input channel) scaling factor that minimizes the output difference after quantization for a certain layer. Formally, we want to optimize the following objective:\n\n\n\n\n\n\nwhich in other words means:\n\n\n\n\nSimilarly to what we said in GPTQ, also here the author care for finding a scale for the entier channel, that is why the diag(s)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe activations are insteda scaled down but the same reasoning applies.\n\nSince the quantization function is not differentiable, they were not able to directly optimize the problem with vanilla backpropagation. So ther define a search space for the optimal scale by analyzing the factors that will affect the choice of scaling factor. \nBut a layer with 4096 input channels would have:\n\n\n\nmeaning 4096 independent optimization variables. That is expensive!\n\nAWQ's trick is: given that the saliency of weight channels is actually determined by the activation scale (thus “activation-awareness”) do not search over arbitrary scales but build the scales directly from activation statistics.\n\nThey compute:\n\n\n\nwhich is the average magnitude of activation (per-channel), and they use a single hyper-parameter α to balance between the protection of salient and non-salient channels. \n\n\n\n\nThey can find the best α by a fast grid search over the interval of [0, 1] (0 means we do not scale; 1 corresponds to the most aggressive scaling in our search space).\n\nThe whole algorithm looks like this:\n\n    1. Calibration data\n    2. measure activation magnitude\n    3. sX\n    4. try different α values\n    5. s = sX^α\n    6. quantize weights\n    7. measure output error\n    8. choose best α\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
