    {
      "id": "zX7Yo-PxNajsShv-Uk0Dh",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -4969.53472471478,
      "y": 2702.4363333420497,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 2875,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "1FILJtSBf9kFoHr3g2O9S",
        "3vNaVc3BvB_nZg2ue1P1L",
        "h5MmbBFjOnSwpDC6Zl_xq"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bPJ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 592578554,
      "version": 4584,
      "versionNonce": 1757330470,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789027095060,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "So now the authors are able to identify the salient weght in the neural network. But they still have to\nget rid of the FP16 to have a further optimization.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nLet's rehearse the quantization formualas keeping the notation in the paper:\n\n\n\n\n\n\nwhere N is the number of quantization bits, and ∆ is the quantization scaler determined by the absolute\nmaximumvalue.\n\nThis next equation is the central trick of AWQ. The author founds that moltiplying the weghts by a\nfactor S protected it from quantization degratadio. In this way they managed to get rid of the FP16\nwhile keeping performance.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nLet's see how it works with an example.\nA linear layer computes: y = wx\nwith w=0.15 and x=10\nTherefore: y = 0.15 × 10 = 1.5\nThis is the FP16/FP32 result.\n\nAssume our quantizer has: Δ=0.1\nThe quantization rule is: \n\n    Q(0.15)=0.1×Round(1.5) = 0.2\n\nThe quantized output becomes: y^​=Q(w)x = 2\nThe error is: 2−1.5=0.5\n\n\nNow we multiply the weight by s=2\n\nThe new weight is:\n\n    Q(w′) = Q(w⋅s) = 0.1 × round(0.15×2 / 0.1) = 0.3\n\nBut this would change the computation:\n    \n    0.30×10=3\n\nThat is why we must compensate the activation:\n    \n    x′ = x/s​ = 5\n\nNow: y^​=Q(w′)x′ =0.3×5 =1.5\n\nThis seems magic! ✨✨ Scaling up makes the channel behave like FP16 even if quantized!\nWhy does this work?\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nNow, the quantization error from equation 1 and 2 can be expressed as\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe ratio of the new error to the original error is\n\n\n\n\n\nGiven ∆' ≈ ∆ and s > 1, the relative error is smaller for the salient weight w. In other words s scale down\nthe error.\n\nTo verify the idea, we multiply the 1% salient channels with s > 1 for the OPT-6.7B model. We find that\nscaling up the salient channels is quite effective: the perplexity improves from 23.54 for s = 1 (simply\nRTN) to 11.92 for s = 2. As s goes larger, the percentage of changed ∆ generally gets larger, but the\npercentage is still quite small for s < 2. Nonetheless, the best PPL actually appears at s = 2. This is\nbecause if we use a very large s, it will increase the relative error for the nonsalient channels when ∆\nincreases (the error of non-salient channels will be amplified by",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "So now the authors are able to identify the salient weght in the neural network. But they still have to get rid of the FP16 to have a further optimization.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nLet's rehearse the quantization formualas keeping the notation in the paper:\n\n\n\n\n\n\nwhere N is the number of quantization bits, and ∆ is the quantization scaler determined by the absolute maximumvalue.\n\nThis next equation is the central trick of AWQ. The author founds that moltiplying the weghts by a factor S protected it from quantization degratadio. In this way they managed to get rid of the FP16 while keeping performance.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nLet's see how it works with an example.\nA linear layer computes: y = wx\nwith w=0.15 and x=10\nTherefore: y = 0.15 × 10 = 1.5\nThis is the FP16/FP32 result.\n\nAssume our quantizer has: Δ=0.1\nThe quantization rule is: \n\n    Q(0.15)=0.1×Round(1.5) = 0.2\n\nThe quantized output becomes: y^​=Q(w)x = 2\nThe error is: 2−1.5=0.5\n\n\nNow we multiply the weight by s=2\n\nThe new weight is:\n\n    Q(w′) = Q(w⋅s) = 0.1 × round(0.15×2 / 0.1) = 0.3\n\nBut this would change the computation:\n    \n    0.30×10=3\n\nThat is why we must compensate the activation:\n    \n    x′ = x/s​ = 5\n\nNow: y^​=Q(w′)x′ =0.3×5 =1.5\n\nThis seems magic! ✨✨ Scaling up makes the channel behave like FP16 even if quantized!\nWhy does this work?\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nNow, the quantization error from equation 1 and 2 can be expressed as\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe ratio of the new error to the original error is\n\n\n\n\n\nGiven ∆' ≈ ∆ and s > 1, the relative error is smaller for the salient weight w. In other words s scale down the error.\n\nTo verify the idea, we multiply the 1% salient channels with s > 1 for the OPT-6.7B model. We find that scaling up the salient channels is quite effective: the perplexity improves from 23.54 for s = 1 (simply RTN) to 11.92 for s = 2. As s goes larger, the percentage of changed ∆ generally gets larger, but the percentage is still quite small for s < 2. Nonetheless, the best PPL actually appears at s = 2. This is because if we use a very large s, it will increase the relative error for the nonsalient channels when ∆ increases (the error of non-salient channels will be amplified by",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
